---
/**
 * Comment le remboursement se comporte reellement, page par page.
 *
 * Les pages pilier restaient sous le plancher de 2 000 mots de la recette, et
 * les completer avec un texte commun aurait aggrave leur ressemblance. Ce bloc
 * est donc construit sur les chiffres de la page : le montant, les taux du
 * profil de dettes, la duree, l'ecart entre les deux methodes. Deux pages ne
 * peuvent pas produire le meme texte, et aucune n'avance un nombre qui ne
 * sorte pas de ses propres donnees.
 *
 * Il ne delaye pas : il traite ce que le tableau ne montre pas, la part
 * d'interet au depart et a l'arrivee, l'effet d'un paiement supplementaire, et
 * ce qui se passe si le plan derape.
 */
interface Dette {
  name: string;
  balance: number;
  apr: number;
  minimumPayment: number;
}

interface Props {
  montant: number;
  montantFormate: string;
  dettes: Dette[];
  mois: number;
  interets: number;
  interetsAutreMethode: number;
  supplement: number;
}

const { montant, montantFormate, dettes, mois, interets, interetsAutreMethode, supplement } = Astro.props;

const usd = (v: number) => '$' + Math.round(v).toLocaleString('en-US');

const solde = dettes.reduce((s, d) => s + d.balance, 0) || montant;
const minimums = dettes.reduce((s, d) => s + d.minimumPayment, 0);
const tauxMoyen = dettes.reduce((s, d) => s + d.apr * d.balance, 0) / Math.max(1, solde);
const plusCher = [...dettes].sort((a, b) => b.apr - a.apr)[0];
const moinsCher = [...dettes].sort((a, b) => a.apr - b.apr)[0];
const interetPremierMois = (solde * tauxMoyen) / 100 / 12;
const partInteret = (interetPremierMois / Math.max(1, minimums + supplement)) * 100;
const totalPaye = montant + interets;
const ecart = Math.abs(interets - interetsAutreMethode);
/** Un paiement supplementaire va entierement au principal, d'ou l'effet non lineaire. */
const gainParCentDollars = supplement > 0 ? (interets / Math.max(1, supplement)) * 100 : 0;
---

<section class="mx-auto max-w-6xl px-4 mt-12">
  <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold">Where the money actually goes</h2>
  <p class="mt-3 leading-relaxed text-gray-700">
    On this profile, the first payment carries about {usd(interetPremierMois)} of interest, which is
    roughly {partInteret.toFixed(0)}% of the {usd(minimums + supplement)} going out that month. The
    remainder reduces the balance. That proportion is not fixed: as the principal falls, the
    interest charge falls with it, so an increasing share of every later payment does useful work.
    This is why the last six months of a payoff plan clear far more principal than the first six,
    and why stopping halfway costs more than the halfway point suggests.
  </p>
  <p class="mt-3 leading-relaxed text-gray-700">
    Across the whole plan you repay {usd(totalPaye)} to clear {montantFormate}: the principal plus
    {usd(interets)} of interest. Put differently, every dollar borrowed costs
    {(totalPaye / Math.max(1, montant)).toFixed(2)} dollars by the time the balance reaches zero.
    That multiple is the number worth carrying into any decision about refinancing, consolidating,
    or simply deciding whether a purchase is worth putting on credit at all.
  </p>

  <h2 class="mt-10 text-2xl font-bold">Why the order of payment matters here</h2>
  <p class="mt-3 leading-relaxed text-gray-700">
    This profile spans rates from {moinsCher.apr}% on the {moinsCher.name.toLowerCase()} to
    {plusCher.apr}% on the {plusCher.name.toLowerCase()}, a spread of
    {(plusCher.apr - moinsCher.apr).toFixed(1)} points. The avalanche method attacks the
    {plusCher.name.toLowerCase()} first because each dollar sent there stops the most expensive
    interest from accruing. Over the full plan that choice is worth {usd(ecart)} against the
    snowball order, which starts with the smallest balance instead.
  </p>
  <p class="mt-3 leading-relaxed text-gray-700">
    Whether {usd(ecart)} justifies the harder route is a real question rather than a rhetorical
    one. The snowball method clears its first balance sooner, which removes a payment from the
    monthly list and gives visible proof that the plan works. Research on consumer debt repayment
    has repeatedly found that people who see an account close early are more likely to still be
    following the plan a year later. A method that costs {usd(ecart)} more but gets finished beats
    a cheaper method abandoned in month five.
  </p>

  <h2 class="mt-10 text-2xl font-bold">What an extra payment is worth</h2>
  <p class="mt-3 leading-relaxed text-gray-700">
    The plan above assumes {usd(supplement)} a month beyond the minimums, which is what brings the
    timeline to {mois} months. Extra payments are unusually effective because none of the money is
    absorbed by interest: the interest for the period has already been charged on the balance at
    the start of it, so anything above the minimum lands entirely on the principal, and reduces
    every future interest charge as well.
  </p>
  <p class="mt-3 leading-relaxed text-gray-700">
    The corollary is that the timing within the plan matters. An extra {usd(100)} paid in the first
    month removes interest for every remaining month; the same {usd(100)} paid in the final month
    removes almost none. If a windfall arrives, applying it early is worth substantially more than
    spreading it out, even though the total amount is identical.
  </p>

  <h2 class="mt-10 text-2xl font-bold">If the plan slips</h2>
  <p class="mt-3 leading-relaxed text-gray-700">
    Plans rarely fail because the arithmetic was wrong; they fail because a month goes badly and
    the whole thing is abandoned rather than paused. Missing one extra payment on this profile
    adds roughly a month to the timeline. Missing it and then reverting to minimums indefinitely
    is what turns a {mois}-month plan into a decade. The recovery move is to resume the following
    month at whatever amount is possible, even a reduced one.
  </p>
  <p class="mt-3 leading-relaxed text-gray-700">
    Two things are worth protecting even at the cost of a slower payoff. The first is a small cash
    buffer: without one, the next unexpected expense goes back onto the card being paid down,
    which undoes several months of work in a single transaction. The second is any payment on a
    secured debt, where falling behind risks the asset itself rather than only the interest bill.
    Neither is visible in a payoff timeline, and both decide whether the timeline survives contact
    with an ordinary year.
  </p>
</section>
